LOCAL FEATURES:
a.SIFT and ORB: Keypoints Mark krte hai
1. SIFT: SIFT = Scale-Invariant Feature Transform.SIFT finds important keypoints and creates a numerical descriptor for each keypoint.The descriptor captures the local appearance around the keypoint.
SIFT is designed to be relatively robust to:
scale changes
rotation
moderate illumination changes

2.ORB = Oriented FAST and Rotated BRIEF
It provides:
keypoint detection
orientation information
binary descriptors
ORB is generally computationally lighter than SIFT and is very useful for real-time applications

HOG = Histogram of Oriented Gradients
It describes an object using the directions of its local gradients.

Layer 3

Feature vector mainly contains geometric/shape information:

$$ X_{shape}= [A,P,W,H,AR,Solidity,Circularity] $$
Layer 4

Feature vector can contain multiple visual characteristics:

$$ X= [ Shape, Color, Texture, Gradient, LocalFeatures ] $$

So Layer 4 creates a richer representation.
